Rather than guessing which long-tail keywords are winnable, every candidate below was tested against a live search engine results page (SERP) in August 2026. The research confirmed the core hypothesis from the ranking audit: **regional long-tail keywords have dramatically weaker competition than national head terms**, and in several cases your homepage is *already* surfacing — meaning the moment your rendering fix (from the previous report) goes live, these pages can claim the top positions.

Three discoveries from the SERP checks are worth highlighting. First, for "return loads Rourkela", your homepage already ranks #1 while positions #2–5 are occupied by **UK haulage websites** (returnloads.net, generalhaulageltd.com) that the engine itself flags as missing the "Rourkela" modifier — there is effectively no relevant Indian content competing for this query. Second, for "trucker earnings in Odisha", the entire first page consists of job boards and salary databases (Indeed, Glassdoor, Jooble, trucoi.com); **not a single comprehensive article about actual trucker profitability in Odisha exists**. Third, for "e-way bill rules for transporters 2026" the SERP has only ~7,500 results versus ~58,000 for the head query, and the #1 result plus the AI answer box is held by a small transporter blog (sktranslines.com) — proof that authority is not a barrier on this modifier.

